          "prompt": "MANDATORY: ONE SINGLE HORIZONTAL ROW of exactly four separate orthographic views of the SAME object, left to right: 1 FRONT view; 2 LEFT side view (object turned so its front points to the LEFT edge of the image); 3 BACK view; 4 RIGHT side view (object turned so its front points to the RIGHT edge of the image). Same scale and same ground line in all four, wide empty gaps between the views, every view complete and not cropped. Pure flat white background. No floor, no ground shadow, no text, no labels, no frames, no perspective, no extra objects.\n\nSTYLE: stylized hand-painted 3D game environment asset painted under even neutral white light with true base colours only: no baked lighting, no cast shadows, no ambient-occlusion darkening, no rim light. Thick chunky simplified silhouette, broad clean faceted planes, two or three dominant colour blocks (chalky warm-white lime plaster, ochre mud brick, dull bronze-gold, a faded vermilion accent, sand-yellow sand), soft painted gradients, sparse medium-size wear. No photorealism, no micro-grain, no fine cracks, no realistic erosion detail, no tiny carvings. Physically plausible.\n\nOBJECT, whitewashed bell-shaped stupa (16.6m wide x 19.8m tall x 16.6m deep): A Silk Road Buddhist stupa of the inverted-bowl type: a square two-step plinth with chunky mouldings, on it a round drum, then one big plain bell-shaped dome swelling out wider than the drum, then a small square box on the dome top, then a tall tapering spire of thirteen thick stacked rings, a wide flat parasol disc and a short finial. The dome and plinth are whitewashed lime plaster, chalky warm white, with large worn patches where ochre mud brick shows through, especially low on the plinth; a drift of sand-yellow sand piled against two sides of the plinth. Spire rings dull bronze-gold, parasol disc dull bronze. One faded vermilion band around the drum. Overall proportion: about 0.85 times as wide as tall, the dome about 0.6 of the total width, the spire about 0.4 of the total height. Perfectly symmetric about the vertical axis, so all four views are the same shape; only the worn patches and the sand drift differ. Bold simple volumes, no tiny niches, no carved figures.",
